65,3,2,talk,Asuka,,- **Asuka**: The options are on the table. We must choose one that protects our newcomers while covering the fixed costs.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,- **Benjamin**: Option C is a trap. The hours log proves chasing individual payments destroys the treasurer. We need a yearly collection.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"- **Jonathan**: @Benjamin, I agree on the treasurer's burden. But Option A ignores that eighteen members never show up. They shouldn't pay the same as the core nine.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"-Shunichi: @Jonathan, Option B creates the exact friction we saw in the minute books. We need a simple entry point, not a hierarchy.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Benjamin, Option A is the only one that keeps the books simple. I refuse to fund a system that requires chasing people for money.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Jonathan, I hear you, but a flat fee is the only way to keep newcomers drifting in without a barrier.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Takumi, agreed. The ledger proves fixed costs dominate. A flat fee is the only way to keep the books sane and the treasurer employed.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Asuka, I concede the newcomer point. But eighteen ghosts paying for the core nine’s work is unsustainable. I cannot support A.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@Jonathan, I understand the frustration, but a tiered system creates the exact social friction we are trying to avoid. We need simplicity.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Takumi,,"@Jonathan, I hear you, but the ledger shows fixed costs are seventy percent. We cannot afford to lose that income. Option A is the only stable choice.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Asuka,,I vote for Option A. It is the only structure that keeps the door open for newcomers and keeps the books simple.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"I vote for Option A. The hours log is decisive; we cannot afford the administrative chaos of C, and B is too complex.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,I vote for Option B. I cannot support a flat fee that subsidizes eighteen ghosts while the core nine carry the physical load.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,I vote for Option A. Simplicity preserves the belonging that is currently slipping away.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Takumi,,"I vote for Option A. The numbers are clear, and I am done with the uncertainty. Let's move on.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
